  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied through six innings, then Salas and Tatis arrived in the seventh.",
    "what_this_means": "Nick Pivetta gave them clean air. Four scoreless innings on 7 strikeouts, exiting after 4.2 with nothing in the loss column. The game hung even at one apiece until the seventh, when Salas drove in a run and Tatis added another. That was the break. Yuki Matsui, Jason Adam, Adrian Morejon, and Mason Miller moved through 4.1 innings after, combining for just one run allowed. The Dodgers didn't fold either. They scored once in the eighth when Morejon gave it back. But the Padres held. The structure worked: start clean, wait for the moment, close the door. This is how balanced teams win in September.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tied games decide championships. This team doesn't flinch when the scoreboard says even heading into the late innings. Salas and Tatis converted their window when it opened, and the bullpen did not waste what they built. The pitching floor is real. When the starter exits clean and the pen holds two runs over four innings, the game belongs to whoever can punch first late. The Padres did. Eight left on base, but the four that mattered scored.",
